AB  - Dengue remains a significant public health concern, especially in tropical and subtropical regions, with current diagnostic methods often falling short due to delayed detection, high costs, and limited applicability in resource-constrained settings. This study presents a label-free electrochemical immunosensor for detection of dengue nonstructural protein 1 (NS1), utilizing a titanium dioxide (TiO2) and multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) nanocomposite, combined with protein G. The synergy between TiO2 and MWCNTs enhances conductivity, stability, and sensitivity, while the incorporation of protein G eliminates the need for chemical linking agents. The immunosensor demonstrated a linear detection range of 100 fg mL-1-100 ng mL-1, with an ultra-low limit of detection (LOD) of 33.3 fg mL-1, and excellent stability, selectivity, and reproducibility. By analyzing clinical samples (positive: n = 5; healthy controls: n = 3), the effectiveness of the immunosensor was contrasted with the enzyme linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA), yielding satisfactory findings. This study identifies a potentially useful diagnostic tool with significant clinical applications for the early diagnosis and treatment of dengue infections. Integration of artificial intelligence (AI) and the Internet of Things (IoT) for real-time data analysis and remote monitoring can improve diagnostic skills even more.
