AB  - In this study, a highly sensitive regeneratable surface plasmon resonance (SPR) sensor for the recognition of the dengue virus is proposed and examined. The BK7 prism, Ag, titanium disilicide (TiSi2), black phosphorus (BP), and sensing media are layered sequentially into the SPR sensor's structure. The sensing medium ingests several healthy and dengue-infected blood components, including plasma, platelets, and hemoglobin. The geometrical factors like the thickness of the Ag layer and the number of TiSi2 and BP layers are rationally tuned to achieve high performance. The suggested SPR-based dengue sensor achieves maximum sensitivity of 257.3 deg/RIU, a quality factor of 85.45 deg(-1), and detection accuracy of 0.54 RIU-1. Because of these exceptional sensing abilities, the proposed sensor is useful for identifying the dengue virus. To analyze the electric field strength close to the interface between TiSi2 and the sensing medium, the proposed structure is simulated using the COMSOL Multiphysics tool.
